  "version": 1,
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Changing Workforce and Deadline Problems",
    "description": "Use worker-days for equal-efficiency teams that grow, shrink, or must meet a deadline.",
    "teachingApproach": "Introduce worker-days as a compact unit only after establishing equal efficiency. Build from fixed workforce inverse proportion to joins, leaves, delays, original-headcount recovery, and extra-worker deadline questions. Every problem first checks whether workers are equivalent; if not, it belongs in the mixed-efficiency lesson instead.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 31,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 17, Time and Work. Approved scope: Teach equal-efficiency workforce questions using worker-days. Cover workers joining after part of a job is done, workers leaving and causing delay, finding the original workforce from a delayed finish, hiring extra workers to meet a deadline, and calculating how far behind schedule a team would be without reinforcement. Distinguish these from mixed-efficiency problems: worker-days works directly only when workers are equivalent."
  },
